And so I just want to know how your methodology into that.

00:14:29.409 --> 00:14:31.150
Of course, yeah, so multi-family.

00:14:31.919 --> 00:14:33.070
Multi-family works a little bit different,

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:34.960
especially for schools, because, you know,

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:37.820
single family is kind of considered the traditional family home,

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.479
um, but in a lot of cases we have been noticing with the data that some

00:14:41.479 --> 00:14:43.580
multi-family developments have been having more,

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:47.669
um, more families, and so we, we, we've looked at some of the,

00:14:47.679 --> 00:14:49.719
uh, the multi-family in terms of, you know,

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:53.669
whether or not it's, uh, a multi-unit like multi-story apartment complex

00:14:53.669 --> 00:14:57.159
versus some of the single family for rent properties that you've seen,

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.119
especially off of South Parkway.

00:14:59.469 --> 00:15:01.760
Um, and so we, we've been looking at that,

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:04.559
uh, and, and the, the school system's been very helpful with,

00:15:04.599 --> 00:15:08.479
uh, giving us some data on that, um, on in terms of enrollment and trying

00:15:08.479 --> 00:15:11.799
to figure out how to model how, uh, multi-family,

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.020
uh, works with the, with the enrollment of the,

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.200
uh, of the school system, so.

00:15:18.289 --> 00:15:22.609
So a follow up to that question, what is the math for if you have a certificate

00:15:22.609 --> 00:15:25.469
of occupancy, whether it's single family or multi-family?

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:30.289
What's the math that translates into we're predicting X number of children

00:15:30.289 --> 00:15:31.510
will be coming to the school?

00:15:31.609 --> 00:15:36.090
Sure, so when we're, when we're doing population and enrollment projections,

00:15:36.169 --> 00:15:37.690
typically our rule of thumb is.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:43.359
Single family will have 9 out of 90% of single family homes are usually

00:15:43.359 --> 00:15:46.330
occupied, whereas multi-family, it's probably closer to 80%.

00:15:46.429 --> 00:15:50.119
Um, there's a lot more fluidity when it comes to multi-family units.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:53.750
Um, so that's what we use on that, um, when we come,

00:15:53.919 --> 00:15:59.340
when it comes to the number of, uh, number of occupants per unit,

00:15:59.609 --> 00:16:01.320
uh, which is, is pretty important for the,

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:02.659
for those projections as well.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.440
Once again, single family tends to have a higher number,

00:16:05.559 --> 00:16:08.419
probably closer to 2.5 persons per household,

00:16:08.719 --> 00:16:13.320
um, whereas a, uh, multi-family unit probably has somewhere between 1.5

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:14.059
and 2.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:15.919
it depends on the area as well.

00:16:16.039 --> 00:16:19.880
Like Research Park, we would expect to have a lower person's occupancy per

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:20.419
household.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.909
Um, just because you have a lot of, uh, singles or,

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:27.830
or, you know, couples that live in like mid-city and Research Park,

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.270
whereas a place like South Huntsville or Hampton Cove,

00:16:30.599 --> 00:16:32.880
even in a multi-family complex, it may be a little bit higher.

00:16:33.159 --> 00:16:34.359
So yeah, that's how we project those.

00:16:34.599 --> 00:16:38.919
